Z||000000||mikabika||08-15-2001||07:05 AM||||Pisa Corp sells wheel adapter plates to change the 5x100mm bolt pattern, to a more common 5x4.5 pattern. Has anyone ever tried these? What would some of the repercussions be if I put these on my '87 GT? TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||08-15-2001||01:02 PM||||The wheels are going to stick out at least 1 1/4 inches so you will have to take that into account when getting new wheels.I dont know of any other problems that could come up with useing adapters.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||08-15-2001||05:33 PM||||DONT USE THEM. Ive seen them break apart and send your car flying out of control, unfortunately usually on a curve. Going around a curve at 65 mph is not a good time for your wheel to decide to leave your car.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52976111"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||rockcrawl||08-15-2001||06:28 PM||||I don't see a problem with using them as long as they are well made and machined from a high strength material. Don't use cheap cast adapters, billet only.<P>Hey, if it isn't safe, they wouldn't sell it....right?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||08-15-2001||10:36 PM||||LOL Rockcrawl, ya like Pintos, Geo Trackers, and Firestone tires.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||cadaver||08-17-2001||12:19 AM||||I would never use adapters. If I wanted to change the pattern I would either redrill the rotors and hubs if there is enough meat or get some made.||||reg||1
